      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the cost of a pencil, expressed in cents. Let's denote the cost of one pen as \\( p \\) cents and the cost of one pencil as \\( q \\) cents. We are given two equations based on the problem statement:\n\n1. Four pens and three pencils cost \\$2.24, which is equivalent to 224 cents.\n   \\[\n   4p + 3q = 224\n   \\]\n\n2. Two pens and five pencils cost \\$1.54, which is equivalent to 154 cents.\n   \\[\n   2p + 5q = 154\n   \\]\n\nWe now have a system of linear equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n4p + 3q = 224 \\\\\n2p + 5q = 154\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nTo eliminate one of the variables, we can use the method of elimination. First, we multiply the first equation by 2 to align the coefficients of \\( p \\):\n\\[\n2(4p + 3q) = 2(224) \\implies 8p + 6q = 448\n\\]\n\nNext, we subtract the second equation from this new equation:\n\\[\n(8p + 6q) - (2p + 5q) = 448 - 154 \\implies 6p + q = 294\n\\]\n\nNow we have a new equation:\n\\[\n6p + q = 294\n\\]\n\nWe can solve for \\( q \\) by isolating it in this equation:\n\\[\nq = 294 - 6p\n\\]\n\nWe substitute this expression for \\( q \\) back into the original first equation:\n\\[\n4p + 3(294 - 6p) = 224\n\\]\n\nDistribute the 3:\n\\[\n4p + 882 - 18p = 224\n\\]\n\nCombine like terms:\n\\[\n-14p + 882 = 224\n\\]\n\nIsolate \\( p \\):\n\\[\n-14p = 224 - 882 \\implies -14p = -658 \\implies p = \\frac{-658}{-14} \\implies p = 47\n\\]\n\nNow that we have \\( p = 47 \\), we substitute this value back into the expression for \\( q \\):\n\\[\nq = 294 - 6(47) = 294 - 282 = 12\n\\]\n\nThus, the cost of a pencil is \\( 12 \\) cents. The final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{12}\n\\]",
